# Kubernetes & Vault Cluster Chaos Engineering Test Plan

Test 1: [Pod Level Failures - Cover reaction of wordpress, mariadb, vault injector, ingress nginx, helm, flux, prometheus, exporters]

## Pod Operations:
    - We need to see the application wordpress stability while [Browsing + Uploading Videos] and we do operations on the cluster led to:
        - pod down and up "restart"
        - pod down and up "recreate"
        - pod down and up "force delete"
        - 2 pods down intentionally out of the scale of 3 
        - 3 pods down intentionally out of the scale of 3
        - Scale up and Scale down automation based on the load and status
        - Customer upload video while checking from nginx which node/pod they're on and tear it down mid-way upload
        - Check if mid-upload video data persists, can be resumed, or if DB transaction fails with duplicate name conflict
        - Monitor pod restart patterns and CrashLoopBackOff scenarios
        - Test pod eviction under memory/CPU pressure
        - What pods have priority in scheduling? What happens to flux? helm? db? and app pods during resource contention?

# Worker Node Level:
    - We need to see the application wordpress stability while we do operations on the cluster led to:
        - worker node down and up "restart"
        - worker node down and up "force shutdown"
        - 2 worker nodes down intentionally out of the scale of 3 
        - 3 worker nodes down intentionally out of the scale of 3
        - Disconnect worker node from nfs while pod is running with no actions on web server and then recover the connection and check if the pods are running or not
        - Repeat with disconnect the nfs connection itself on all nodes and see if it can be recovered auto or will hang forever 
        - Will the uploaded mid-way data exist or disappear, will it be able to upload same file or db will crash because of mid-way transaction fail with same name 
        - Recover db after mid transaction fail if any
        - Test Node NotReady vs actual node down - how long until pods are rescheduled?
        - Customer upload video, check from nginx which worker node they're on, tear down that specific worker mid-upload and verify behavior
        - Monitor node resource usage (CPU/mem) during adjacent node failures from Grafana

# Master Node Level:
    - We need to see the application wordpress stability while we do operations on the cluster led to:
    - Test Cluster Stability and ETCD survival, restore its backup 
        - master node down and up "restart"
        - master node down and up "force shutdown"
        - 2 master nodes down intentionally out of the scale of 3 
        - 3 master nodes down intentionally out of the scale of 3
        - Force stop 2 nodes and check if the app is working or not and then recover the nodes and check if the app is working or not and etcd
        - Force stop 3 nodes and check if the app is working or not and then recover the nodes and check if the app is working or not and etcd
        - Tear down 1 master node and monitor from Grafana if it puts load on the other masters in terms of mem and cpu
        - Test API server availability during master failures - can kubectl commands execute?
        - Test scheduler and controller-manager behavior with reduced master quorum

# Combined Failure Scenarios (Multi-Component):
    - Critical combined failure testing:
        - 2 workers down + 1 master down - check app stability, pod rescheduling, what happens to flux? helm? db? app?
        - 2 workers down + 2 masters down - verify minimum viable cluster state
        - 2 workers down + 3 masters down - expect total control plane failure
        - 3 workers down + 1 master down - all worker capacity gone, control plane partially available
        - What is the pod priority order during these combined failures? Which workloads survive and which get evicted?

# Vault Level:
    - We need to see the application wordpress stability while we do operations on the cluster led to:
        - vault down and up "restart"
        - vault down and up "force shutdown"
        - 2 vault nodes down intentionally out of the scale of 3 
        - 3 vault nodes down intentionally out of the scale of 3
        - Tear down 2 vault nodes led the cluster to lose quorum and see if current pods can hold secrets and new pods can fetch or not 
        - Tear down 3 vault nodes led the cluster to lose quorum and see if current pods can hold secrets and new pods can fetch or not
        - Test Vault seal/unseal cycles - manual seal and auto-unseal behavior
        - Break the unseal auto with AWS for missing or changed secrets and show how to recover the vault and check if the app is working or not
        - Test existing pods with cached secrets vs new pod deployments during Vault outage
        - Verify vault-injector sidecar behavior when Vault cluster is degraded
